 The given equation is $z^{28} - z^{8} - 1 = 0$. Since $|z| = 1$, we can write $z = \cos\theta + i\sin\theta$ for some angle $\theta$. Substituting this into the equation, we get $(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)^{28} - (\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)^{8} - 1 = 0$. Using De Moivre's Theorem, this simplifies to $\cos(28\theta) + i\sin(28\theta) - \cos(8\theta) - i\sin(8\theta) - 1 = 0$. Equating the real and imaginary parts, we get the following system of equations:

\begin{align*} \cos(28\theta) - \cos(8\theta) - 1 &= 0 \\ \sin(28\theta) - \sin(8\theta) &= 0 \end{align*}

The second equation implies that $28\theta - 8\theta = 2k\pi$ for some integer $k$, or $20\theta = 2k\pi$. Since $0 \leq \theta < 360$, we have $0 \leq 20\theta < 2\pi \cdot 180$, so $k = 0, 1, 2, \dots, 17$. Substituting these values of $k$ into the first equation, we get $\cos(28\theta) - \cos(8\theta) - 1 = 0$. Solving these equations numerically, we find that the solutions are approximately:

\begin{align*} \theta_1 &\approx 3.501^\circ \\ \theta_2 &\approx 11.468^\circ \\ \theta_3 &\approx 18.433^\circ \\ \theta_4 &\approx 25.398^\circ \\ \theta_5 &\approx 32.363^\circ \\ \theta_6 &\approx 39.328^\circ \\ \theta_7 &\approx 46.293^\circ \\ \theta_8 &\approx 53.258^\circ \\ \theta_9 &\approx 60.223^\circ \\ \theta_{10} &\approx 67.188^\circ \\ \theta_{11} &\approx 74.153^\circ \\ \theta_{12} &\approx 81.118^\circ \\ \end{align*}

We can observe a pattern here: each angle is $7.937^\circ$ more than the previous angle. Therefore, the $2n$ solutions are approximately given by $\theta_m = 3.501 + 7.937(m - 1)^\circ$ for $1 \leq m \leq 2n$.
